<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jōdo Shinshū</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jodo Shinshu, Shin Buddhism, True Pure Land school, Ikkō-shū</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jōdo Shinshū, the True Pure Land school, is a school of Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinran">Shinran</a> in the 13th century, teaching salvation through faith in the Buddha <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a>. Revived by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennyo">Rennyo</a> in the 15th century, its Hongan-ji organisation won mass support among farmers and townspeople and led the armed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki">Ikko-ikki</a> leagues. It remains the largest Buddhist school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jodo-shinshu.jpg" alt="Jōdo Shinshū" width="320" height="324" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shinran_Shonin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
